      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "UNLABELLED",
          "content": "Fungus-induced asthmatic disease is characterized by persistent airway hyperreactivity and remodeling."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE AND DESIGN",
          "content": "To determine the role of IL-18 in the allergic airway response to Aspergillus fumigatus conidia in a murine model of A. fumigatus-induced asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A. fumigatus-sensitized mice were depleted of IL-18 using a polyclonal anti-IL-18 antibody for 3 days after a conidia challenge."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Airway hyperresponsiveness and eosinophil numbers were significantly elevated 3-30 days after conidia challenge compared to the normal serum-treated group. Histological evidence showed retention of A. fumigatus conidia, airway remodeling, subepithelial fibrosis, and increased collagen deposition in the lungs of IL-18-depleted mice at day 30 after the conidia challenge. Prolonged retention of conidia in IL-18 depleted A. fumigatus-sensitized mice was associated with decreased Toll-like receptor-2 (TLR-2) expression compared with the control group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "IL-18 modulates the innate immune response against A. fumigatus conidia and prevents the development of severe fungus-induced asthmatic disease."
        }
      ]},
